IMPORT prelude
IMPORT daydate
IMPORT `ea89-types`
IMPORT `ea89-days`

-- Electricity Act 1989 (UK) — GOAL 2: NON-PAYMENT, AND ENTRY (Schedule 6
-- paras 2, 5, 7(4)-(5), 8(3)-(4) and 10(4)).
-- Source: ../registers/source-bundle/ukpga-1989-29.txt.
-- The customer asks: "Can my supplier fit a pre-payment meter or disconnect
-- me for an unpaid bill, from when; may it enter to do so; and can I restore
-- the connection myself?"

§ `Goal 2 — non-payment`

-- para 2(3): "the period of 28 days after the making by the supplier of a
-- demand in writing". Payment by the demand day + 28 is within it.
@ref Sch 6 para 2(3)
GIVEN c IS A `Non-payment case`
GIVETH A DATE
`the last day of the requisite period for` c MEANS `the day` 28 `days after` (c's `demand made on`)

-- para 2(2)(b): "not less than seven working days' notice has been given to
-- the occupier ... of his intention to exercise it". para 2(1) is met once
-- the 28 days pass unpaid; a later payment does not end the power (F5). The
-- notice may be given at any time after the demand (F6); the power is
-- exercisable from the later of the day after the requisite period and the
-- seventh working day after the notice (F2).
@ref Sch 6 para 2(1)-(3)
@export From what day may my electricity supplier install a pre-payment meter or disconnect my premises for unpaid charges, under Schedule 6 para 2 of the Electricity Act 1989 (UK)? The relevant payments must not have been made within 28 days after the supplier's written demand; the amount must not be genuinely in dispute (a s 39 dispute is disregarded); and at least seven working days' notice of the intention must have been given. The answer is the later of the day after the 28 days and the seventh working day after the notice. NOTHING where there is no such power or no notice. DRAFT, not reviewed by a UK lawyer.
GIVEN c IS A `Non-payment case`        @desc The demand and any payment, any dispute, the notice of intention, and the action and its day
      holidays IS A LIST OF DATE        @desc The bank holidays
GIVETH A MAYBE DATE
`the earliest day for a pre-payment meter or disconnection for` c `, bank holidays` holidays MEANS
    IF     c's `genuinely in dispute`
        OR `paid in time` c
    THEN NOTHING
    ELSE
        CONSIDER c's `notice of intention given on`
        WHEN NOTHING THEN NOTHING
        WHEN JUST n  THEN
            IF n LESS THAN (c's `demand made on`)
            THEN NOTHING
            ELSE JUST (`the later of` (`the day` 1 `days after` (`the last day of the requisite period for` c))
                                      (`the` 7 `th working day after` n `, bank holidays` holidays))
    WHERE
        `paid in time` x MEANS
            CONSIDER x's `paid on`
            WHEN NOTHING THEN FALSE
            WHEN JUST p  THEN p AT MOST `the last day of the requisite period for` x

@ref Sch 6 para 2
@export May my electricity supplier install a pre-payment meter or disconnect my premises on the day it did (or proposes to), under Schedule 6 para 2 of the Electricity Act 1989 (UK), and so recover its expenses from me? DRAFT, not reviewed by a UK lawyer.
GIVEN c IS A `Non-payment case`, holidays IS A LIST OF DATE
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the supplier may act on the day for` c `, bank holidays` holidays IF
    CONSIDER `the earliest day for a pre-payment meter or disconnection for` c `, bank holidays` holidays
    WHEN NOTHING THEN FALSE
    WHEN JUST e  THEN c's `action on` AT LEAST e

§§ `Entry (paras 7(4)-(5), 8(3)-(4), 10(4))`

-- para 7(4)-(5): entry to install a pre-payment meter, with at least two
-- working days' notice. para 8(3)(a), (4): entry to disconnect where the
-- supplier is authorised to disconnect, with at least two working days'
-- notice. para 10(4): on production of a document showing authority. Both
-- "at all reasonable times". The entry is on the day of the action.
@ref Sch 6 paras 7(4)-(5), 8(3)(a), (4), 10(4)
@export May my electricity supplier's officer enter my premises to install a pre-payment meter or disconnect them for non-payment, under Schedule 6 paras 7, 8 and 10 of the Electricity Act 1989 (UK)? Only if the supplier may act that day under para 2, at a reasonable time, with at least two working days' notice of the entry, and on production of a document showing the officer's authority. DRAFT, not reviewed by a UK lawyer.
GIVEN c IS A `Non-payment case`, holidays IS A LIST OF DATE
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the officer may enter for` c `, bank holidays` holidays IF
        `the supplier may act on the day for` c `, bank holidays` holidays
    AND c's `entry at a reasonable time`
    AND c's `authority document produced`
    AND enough
    WHERE
        enough MEANS
            CONSIDER c's `entry notice given on`
            WHEN NOTHING THEN FALSE
            WHEN JUST n  THEN c's `action on` AT LEAST `the` 2 `th working day after` n `, bank holidays` holidays

§§ `Restoring the connection (para 5)`

-- para 5(1)-(2): after a disconnection by the supplier (other than under s 29
-- regulations), no person shall restore the connection without its consent.
@ref Sch 6 para 5
@export Would restoring my electricity connection myself after the supplier disconnected it be an offence under Schedule 6 para 5 of the Electricity Act 1989 (UK)? Yes, unless the supplier consents. DRAFT, not reviewed by a UK lawyer.
GIVEN c IS A `Non-payment case`  @desc Whether the supplier has consented to the connection being restored
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `restoring the connection myself is an offence for` c IF
    NOT (c's `the supplier's consent to restore`)
